WEBVTT

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:01.825
Mistral just made a model

00:00:01.625 --> 00:00:03.638
with about a trillion parameters

00:00:03.438 --> 00:00:04.514
free to download.

00:00:04.554 --> 00:00:05.952
The catch is running it.

00:00:06.652 --> 00:00:08.580
At FP8, the weights alone

00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:10.308
come to roughly a terabyte.

00:00:10.348 --> 00:00:11.630
For a home lab,

00:00:11.430 --> 00:00:13.000
the honest answer is no.

00:00:13.550 --> 00:00:15.166
A router sends each token

00:00:14.966 --> 00:00:16.581
through four of a hundred

00:00:16.381 --> 00:00:17.933
and twenty-eight experts.

00:00:17.973 --> 00:00:20.618
Only about forty-nine billion parameters

00:00:20.418 --> 00:00:21.316
work at once.

00:00:21.866 --> 00:00:24.154
Mistral says it scores forty-eight

00:00:23.954 --> 00:00:25.738
percent on a coding agent

00:00:25.538 --> 00:00:27.610
index, level with a leading

00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:30.273
Chinese model, using forty-seven percent

00:00:30.073 --> 00:00:31.065
fewer tokens.

00:00:31.615 --> 00:00:33.290
But nearly every launch score

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:34.589
comes from Mistral itself.

00:00:34.629 --> 00:00:36.732
One independent index rates it

00:00:36.532 --> 00:00:39.734
twenty-six, well below closed frontier models.

00:00:40.284 --> 00:00:42.713
Mistral says it refuses ninety-six

00:00:42.513 --> 00:00:44.712
point six percent of malicious

00:00:44.512 --> 00:00:45.712
cyber requests.

00:00:45.752 --> 00:00:47.635
Yet a security vendor still

00:00:47.435 --> 00:00:49.668
saw potential to help real attacks.

00:00:50.218 --> 00:00:51.958
Once weights are public,

00:00:51.758 --> 00:00:54.100
anyone can fine-tune the refusals away.

00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:56.330
Guardrail updates only reach people

00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:57.294
who download them.

00:00:57.844 --> 00:00:59.294
Try it hosted first,

00:00:59.094 --> 00:01:00.150
on your own code.

00:01:00.190 --> 00:01:01.516
Read the licence,

00:01:01.316 --> 00:01:02.708
since selling it as a

00:01:02.508 --> 00:01:04.497
paid model service needs a deal.

00:01:04.537 --> 00:01:06.934
Keep a human approving security tests.

00:01:07.484 --> 00:01:09.080
It is the first Western

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:10.917
open model that credibly sits

00:01:10.717 --> 00:01:12.167
at the top for coding.

00:01:12.207 --> 00:01:14.090
Independent tests in the coming

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:15.150
weeks will show if it

00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:15.773
stays there.

